%I A005939 M4612 #43 Oct 31 2026 03:00:12
%S A005939 9,33,91,99,259,451,481,561,657,703,909,1233,1729,2409,2821,2981,3333,
%T A005939 3367,4141,4187,4521,5461,6533,6541,6601,7107,7471,7777,8149,8401,
%U A005939 8911,10001,11111,11169,11649,12403,12801,13833,13981,14701,14817,14911,15211
%N A005939 Pseudoprimes to base 10.
%C A005939 This sequence is a subsequence of A121014 & A121912. In fact the terms are composite terms k of these sequences such that gcd(k,10) = 1. Theorem: If both numbers q & 2q-1 are primes(q is in the sequence A005382) and k = q*(2q-1) then 10^(k-1) == 1 (mod k) (k is in the sequence A005939) iff mod(q, 20) is in the set {1, 7, 19}. 91,703,12403,38503,79003,188191,269011,... are such terms. - _Farideh Firoozbakht_, Sep 15 2006
%C A005939 Composite numbers k such that 10^(k-1) == 1 (mod k). - _Michel Lagneau_, Feb 18 2012
%C A005939 Composite numbers k such that the number of digits of the period of 1/k divides k-1. - _Davide Rotondo_, Dec 16 2020
%D A005939 Richard K. Guy, Unsolved Problems in Number Theory, 3rd Edition, Springer, 2004, Section A12, pp. 44-50.
%D A005939 N. J. A. Sloane and Simon Plouffe, The Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences, Academic Press, 1995 (includes this sequence).
%H A005939 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A005939/b005939.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a> (terms 1..1000 from T. D. Noe)
%H A005939 Carl Pomerance & N. J. A. Sloane, <a href="/A001567/a001567_4.pdf">Correspondence, 1991</a>.
%H A005939 <a href="/index/Ps#pseudoprimes">Index entries for sequences related to pseudoprimes</a>.
%t A005939 Select[Range[15300], ! PrimeQ[ # ] && PowerMod[10, (# - 1), # ] == 1 &] (* _Farideh Firoozbakht_, Sep 15 2006 *)
%o A005939 (PARI) isok(k) = k > 1 && Mod(10, k)^(k-1) == 1 && !isprime(k); \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 31 2026
%Y A005939 Cf. A001567 (pseudoprimes to base 2), A005382, A121014, A121912.
%K A005939 nonn,changed
%O A005939 1,1
%A A005939 _N. J. A. Sloane_
